    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#osman-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/osman-i.jpg" alt="Osman I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#osman-i">Osman I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I_Osman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ottoman miniature painting, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ottoman Empire originated in the fractured political landscape of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the late thirteenth century, where the collapse of Seljuk authority following the Mongol victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-kose-dag">Battle of Köse Dağ</a> in 1243 had fragmented <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia-minor">Asia Minor</a> into a constellation of competing Turkic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#beylik">beyliks</a>, petty principalities established along the shrinking Byzantine frontier. Among these, the beylik of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#osman-i">Osman I</a>, a Turkoman chieftain traditionally said to belong to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kay-tribe">Kayı tribe</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#oghuz-turks">Oghuz Turks</a>, lay in the Bithynian borderlands near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bursa">Bursa</a>, directly on the frontier between the Islamic and Byzantine worlds. This position attracted ghazi warriors, fighters drawn to campaigning against non-Muslim powers, and gave the nascent state a continuous supply of manpower and ideological purpose. Osman’s victory over a Byzantine force at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-bapheus">Battle of Bapheus</a> in 1302 established his military credibility, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#orhan">Orhan</a> captured Bursa in 1326, making it the dynasty’s first true capital and beginning the transition from raiding confederation to territorial state. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fall-of-constantinople">conquest of Constantinople</a> on 29 May 1453 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>, achieved after a siege of some fifty-three days in which great bronze <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#field-artillery">cannon</a> cast by the founder Urban breached the Theodosian walls that had resisted assault for over a thousand years, was the empire’s defining transformation: it extinguished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> after eleven centuries, cast the Ottomans as heirs to Roman imperial prestige and gave them a capital commanding the junction of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#selim-i">Selim I</a>’s conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamluk-sultanate">Mamluk Sultanate</a> in 1516–1517 brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a> and the holy cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mecca">Mecca</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#medina">Medina</a> under Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and the dynasty’s assumption of the caliphal title, traditionally said to have passed from the last Abbasid shadow <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cairo">Cairo</a>, strengthened its claim to leadership of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Islamic world.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#istanbul"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/istanbul.jpg" alt="Istanbul" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#istanbul">Istanbul</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Historical_peninsula_and_modern_skyline_of_Istanbul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hunanuk, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ottoman economy was among the most complex and geographically extensive of the early modern world, encompassing the agricultural surpluses of the Anatolian and Balkan heartlands, the commercial revenues of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#black-sea">Black Sea</a> trade networks, and the fiscal resources of a population that at its sixteenth-century height is estimated at roughly 25–30 million across three continents. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#timar">timar</a> system, the empire’s foundational agrarian-military institution, assigned revenues from specific agricultural lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sipahi">sipahi</a> cavalrymen in exchange for military service, fusing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#land-tenure">land tenure</a>, taxation and military organisation into a single mechanism. It functioned well during the classical period but declined from the late sixteenth century as military needs shifted towards salaried infantry and the state turned increasingly to tax-farming. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#istanbul">Istanbul</a> grew by the later sixteenth century into one of the world’s largest cities, with a population often estimated at several hundred thousand and perhaps as many as 700,000, serving as the nerve centre of an imperial economy that moved grain, textiles, metals and luxury goods across vast territories through a combination of state-directed provisioning and private enterprise. Non-Muslim populations, chiefly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> Christians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armenians">Armenians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jews">Jews</a>, were organised into religious communities, later formalised as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#millet-system">millet system</a>, which enjoyed considerable autonomy in worship, education and matters of personal status. This pragmatic pluralism eased the integration of diverse skilled populations, notably the Sephardic Jews expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a> in 1492, who brought commercial networks and technical expertise such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#printing-press">printing</a> to Ottoman cities. The empire’s control of the principal routes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> gave it great leverage over Eurasian trade, although the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#portuguese-rounding-of-the-cape-of-good-hope">Portuguese rounding of the Cape of Good Hope</a> opened a sea route that diverted part of the spice commerce away from Ottoman-controlled corridors from the early sixteenth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-mohacs"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-mohacs.jpg" alt="Battle of Mohács" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_moh%C3%A1csi_csata_(Des_T%C3%BCrckischen_Keysers_Solymon_schlacht_-_So_mit_dem_K%C3%BCnig_von_Hungern_gethan,_Bamberg,_1526).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ottoman military was among the most innovative and institutionally sophisticated fighting forces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and Middle Eastern world for the better part of three centuries. Its pre-eminence rested on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#janissaries">janissary</a> corps, an elite infantry force recruited through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#devshirme">devshirme</a>, whereby Christian boys, mainly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#balkans">Balkans</a>, were periodically levied, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#islam">Islam</a>, educated and trained as professional soldiers loyal to the sultan rather than to any tribal or feudal allegiance. Organised as a standing salaried infantry and increasingly equipped with firearms from the fifteenth century onwards, the janissaries gave the Ottomans a decisive advantage over opponents relying on feudal cavalry levies. Ottoman artillery was among the most advanced in the world at the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a>’s fall, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>’s deployment of massive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bombard">bombards</a>, including the great gun cast by Urban (or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#orban">Orban</a>), demonstrated a mastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-warfare">siege warfare</a> that the empire refined in later campaigns. The reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> (r. 1520–1566) produced its most ambitious campaigns: the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#belgrade">Belgrade</a> in 1521, the destruction of the Hungarian royal army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-mohacs">Battle of Mohács</a> in 1526, the first siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vienna">Vienna</a> in 1529, and the naval operations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hayreddin-barbarossa">Hayreddin Barbarossa</a>, whose victory at Preveza in 1538 secured Ottoman naval dominance in the eastern and central Mediterranean for a generation. The failed second siege of Vienna in September 1683, when a relief army of imperial German and Polish troops under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jan-iii-sobieski">Jan III Sobieski</a> routed the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#grand-vizier">Grand Vizier</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kara-mustafa-pasha">Kara Mustafa Pasha</a>, marked the end of Ottoman expansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> and opened a long era of territorial retreat that the empire’s military institutions, increasingly resistant to reform, proved unable to reverse.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#suleiman-the-magnificent"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent.jpg" alt="Suleiman the Magnificent" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:EmperorSuleiman.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Circle of Titian, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ottoman administrative system combined Islamic legal tradition, Persian and Byzantine bureaucratic practice and distinctively Ottoman institutions into a governing structure of considerable sophistication, which administered a multi-ethnic, multi-religious empire for over six centuries. The sultan stood at the apex of the state, commanding the military and administrative apparatus through the grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vizier">vizier</a> and the imperial council (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#divan">Divan</a>), while religious and legal affairs were overseen by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sheikh-ul-islam">Sheikh ul-Islam</a> (Şeyhülislam), the empire’s chief religious authority. After the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mamluk">Mamluk</a> lands in 1517 the sultans also claimed the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliph</a>, a claim whose political importance grew markedly in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a>’s legislative activity, which earned him the Ottoman epithet Kanuni, “the Lawgiver”, systematised the relationship between dynastic law (kanun) and Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sharia">sharia</a>, producing codes on criminal justice, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#land-tenure">land tenure</a> and taxation that remained in force for generations. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sublime-porte">Sublime Porte</a>, the grand vizier’s administration, managed a bureaucracy whose sophistication impressed European observers, with specialised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#departement">departments</a> for the treasury, correspondence and provincial affairs, and a hierarchy of governors (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#beylerbey">beylerbeys</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sanjakbey">sancakbeys</a>) that extended the imperial writ from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#crimea">Crimea</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#yemen">Yemen</a> and from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#algeria">Algeria</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tanzimat">Tanzimat</a> reforms of 1839–1876, prompted by growing European military and economic superiority, sought to modernise administration and law, proclaiming equality before the law regardless of religion, reorganising the provinces and founding secular schools, and culminated in the short-lived constitution of 1876. These reforms, however, were implemented unevenly and could not by themselves resolve the empire’s mounting structural problems.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#suleymaniye-mosque"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suleymaniye-mosque.jpg" alt="Süleymaniye Mosque" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#suleymaniye-mosque">Süleymaniye Mosque</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:S%C3%BCleymaniyeMosqueIstanbul_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hunanuk, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ottoman Empire’s global significance was exceptional in its combination of geographical reach, duration and cultural depth, exercising influence across European, Middle Eastern and North African history simultaneously. The fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1453 sent shockwaves through Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> and accelerated the movement of Byzantine scholars, with their Greek <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuscript">manuscripts</a> and classical learning, to Italian cities, a migration that contributed to the intellectual momentum of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#renaissance">Renaissance</a>. Ottoman control of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> and its trade routes is often cited as one of the spurs to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#age-of-discovery">Age of Discovery</a>, the Portuguese and Spanish search for oceanic passages to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a>, so that the consequences of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>’s conquest reached far beyond the region. The long <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-habsburg-wars">Ottoman-Habsburg rivalry</a> structured European geopolitics from the 1520s to the late seventeenth century, with the Ottoman factor repeatedly shaping European alliances, including the Franco-Ottoman alliance concluded under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francis-i">Francis I</a> and Suleiman. In the Islamic world, Ottoman patronage of architecture, poetry, theology and music produced a distinctive imperial aesthetic; the classical style of the architect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mimar-sinan">Mimar Sinan</a>, credited with more than three hundred buildings including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#suleymaniye-mosque">Süleymaniye Mosque</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#istanbul">Istanbul</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#selimiye-mosque">Selimiye Mosque</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#edirne">Edirne</a>, represents one of the most accomplished architectural achievements of the sixteenth century. The empire’s dissolution after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-world-war">First World War</a>, and the subsequent partition of its Arab provinces into British and French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#league-of-nations-mandate">mandates</a>, created state boundaries that departed significantly from Ottoman administrative geography and have shaped the political history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-east">Middle East</a> ever since.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-navarino"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-navarino.jpg" alt="Battle of Navarino" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-navarino">Battle of Navarino</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Navarino.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Ambroise Louis Garneray, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Ottoman Empire’s decline, a concept modern historians increasingly treat as a long process of transformation rather than steady decay, extended over more than two centuries from the failed siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vienna">Vienna</a> in 1683 to the abolition of the sultanate in 1922. It resulted from the combined effects of military setbacks, fiscal strain, nationalist fragmentation and European imperial pressure operating across many fronts. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-karlowitz">Treaty of Karlowitz</a> in 1699, which surrendered most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hungary">Hungary</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#transylvania">Transylvania</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#podolia">Podolia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#poland">Poland</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-kucuk-kaynarca">Treaty of Küçük Kaynarca</a> in 1774, which made the Crimean Khanate independent and gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russia">Russia</a> provisions it later interpreted as a right to protect the sultan’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> subjects, stripped the empire of vital territories and set the precedent of European powers extracting concessions through military pressure. Nineteenth-century nationalist movements among Ottoman Christian populations then progressively dismembered its European provinces: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greece">Greece</a> achieved independence in 1830 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#greek-war-of-independence">Greek War of Independence</a>, which saw British, French and Russian naval intervention at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-navarino">Navarino</a> in 1827, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#romania">Romania</a> gained full independence and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> autonomy after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877–1878, while Armenian, Macedonian and Albanian movements pressed their own claims. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#capitulations-of-the-ottoman-empire">Capitulations</a>, treaty arrangements granting European nationals exemption from Ottoman jurisdiction and favourable tariffs, constrained the empire’s fiscal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sovereignty">sovereignty</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ottoman-public-debt-administration">Ottoman Public Debt Administration</a> established in 1881 placed much of its revenue under European creditor control. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#balkan-wars">Balkan Wars</a> of 1912–1913, which stripped the empire of nearly all its remaining European territory, and its alignment with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#central-powers">Central Powers</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-world-war">First World War</a>, which led to the Allied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#occupation-of-istanbul">occupation of Istanbul</a> from 1918 and the punitive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-sevres">Treaty of Sèvres</a> in 1920, created the conditions for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a>’s nationalist movement. The Grand National Assembly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#abolition-of-the-ottoman-sultanate">abolished the Sultanate</a> on 1 November 1922, proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#republic-of-turkey">Republic of Turkey</a> on 29 October 1923 and abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#caliphate">caliphate</a> in March 1924.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Ottoman Empire stands as one of history’s longest-lived empires, which for over six centuries governed a multi-ethnic, multi-religious realm across three continents and whose dissolution after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-world-war">First World War</a> reshaped the political map of the modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#middle-east">Middle East</a> and south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>.</em></p>

